---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="area-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="area" from="m2" to="ft2" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert an area</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the area in either box. Both boxes convert as you type.</li>
      <li>Pick your units: metric, land units (hectares and acres), or imperial and US squares.</li>
      <li>If you have a length and a width, multiply them first in the same unit, then convert the result. You can also convert each side and then multiply, but that rounds twice.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Area factors are the square of length factors</h2>
    <p>
      This is the most common error. One metre is 3.280839895 feet, so one square metre is
      3.280839895² = 10.76391042 square feet, not 3.28. The same applies to smaller units: 1 m is
      100 cm, but 1 m² is 10,000 cm², and 1 km² is a million square metres, not a thousand.
    </p>
    <p>
      A room of 4 m by 5 m is 20 m², which is 215.28 ft². A common mistake is to convert 20 to 65.6
      with the linear factor, which gives a room three times too small.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Length factor</th><th>Area factor</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 m = 3.280839895 ft</td><td>1 m² = 10.76391042 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 in = 2.54 cm</td><td>1 in² = 6.4516 cm²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 yd = 3 ft</td><td>1 yd² = 9 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 mi = 1,609.344 m</td><td>1 mi² = 2,589,988.11 m²</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Land units: acres, hectares and sections</h2>
    <p>
      An acre is exactly 43,560 square feet, which is 4,046.8564224 m². It comes from medieval field
      measurement (in theory, the area a team of oxen could plough in a day) and has no simple
      relation to metric units. A hectare is a square 100 m on each side, so exactly 10,000 m².
    </p>
    <p>
      1 hectare = 2.4710538 acres, and 1 acre = 0.40468564 hectares. As a rough guide, an acre is a
      little smaller than a standard football pitch and a hectare is a little larger. In the US survey
      system, a square mile is a section and contains exactly 640 acres. For this reason, rural
      American parcels are often 40 or 160 acres.
    </p>

    <h2>Reference: sizes to calibrate against</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Thing</th><th>Metric</th><th>Imperial</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A4 sheet of paper</td><td>0.06237 m²</td><td>0.6713 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Parking space</td><td>12.5 m²</td><td>135 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average UK new-build home</td><td>76 m²</td><td>818 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average US new-build home</td><td>216 m²</td><td>2,325 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tennis court (singles)</td><td>195.6 m²</td><td>2,106 ft²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Football pitch (typical)</td><td>7,140 m²</td><td>1.76 acres</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hectare</td><td>10,000 m²</td><td>2.471 acres</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Buying flooring, paint or turf</h2>
    <p>
      Materials are sold by area, and errors in this arithmetic break budgets. Calculate the area in
      the unit your supplier uses, convert once, then add a waste allowance. Trade practice is 5 % for
      a simple rectangular room, 10 % for diagonal or patterned laying, and 15 % where there are many
      cuts around fixtures. Paint is sold by coverage per litre or per gallon, so convert the wall
      area first and divide. Two coats need twice the paint.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
